-- --- mission 2, The Silo Gambit: D minor implied and never stated: a D2 pedal that enters on step 1 (never the downbeat) and flicks A#2 instead of a third, a chromatic wedge that descends one step further each time it returns (bar 6 stops on B2, bar 10 reaches A#2, bar 19 finally completes the line), an E7b5 floor whose symmetry hides its own root, a G# minor bridge at the tritone pole, and a loop seam where the bass leaps G#2 down to D2 under a hanging A#4. The hook now has a shape and a fixed rhythm — A4(6) A#4(2) A4(4) E4(4) — restated as a real sequence on G4-G#4-G4-D4, as a true inversion up high (E5-D#5-E5-B4, not the descent the old draft mislabelled), and stretched into a two-stage chromatic climb that tops out on F#5 in bar 15, the only F# in the piece and the only moment D turns major. The counter is capped at D#4 so it never crosses or doubles the lead, and it moves in three or four long notes per bar; a formal falling D4-A3-E3 in long-short-long bookends the loop at bars 1 and 19 as the imperial signature. Two deliberate disagreements with the critiques: I kept the F major release consonant rather than dissolving it, but released it by SUBTRACTION — drums out entirely in bar 16, counter tacet, the tune dropped an octave to A4 — so the warmth is an exposure, not a fanfare; and I kept a drum escalation rather than flattening it, because the busiest bars (12-14) now sit under the most dissonant harmony instead of substituting for it, and bar 15 drops to a bare crescendo roll so the loudest thing in the cue is still only a 66-velocity kick. ---

