-- --- mission 3, Static: E minor, 24 bars: the E2 pulse floor now actually MOVES in whole bars, not 16th-note dents (i–VI–bVII–i–V at 9-12, again at 13-16, bII pedal at 17-22, i–V at 23-24), so the counter's fixed F3 beep — the b2, an alien pitch that is never a chord tone — is reinterpreted as b2, then 4th over C, m3 over D, tritone over B, and finally ROOT at bar 17, without ever changing pitch; that is the Reich device the old draft only claimed, and the phasing is real because 16 mod 3 = 1, so counter patterns 0/1/2 continue the 3-step cycle unbroken across bars 3-12 and 20-22 instead of resetting at every barline (the lead's period-5 F5 beep drifts the same way in patterns 3/4/5). The hook is a signal, not a tune: F5–E5–B4, a semitone fall into a tritone, always entering off the downbeat at step 2, and it is never transposed — at bar 17 the floor lurches to F2 while counter and hook STAY in E (E4 rubbing F4, E5 a major seventh, B4 a tritone over the new root), so the semitone shift is a wound rather than a key lift, and bars 20 and 22 fault in TIME as well as pitch (bass rests at 3, 7-8 and 13, drums rotated so the loud hits land on 1/7/13 and nothing at all hits step 0). Two deliberate disagreements: I kept the chromatic glissando's Bb5 because here it sounds where the counter is on F4, not B3, so there is no false relation; and I reconciled "almost no melody" with the ask for a stronger hook by making the hook a three-note siren that recurs identically eight times rather than a theme that develops — the development is all in the floor underneath it. ---

