-- --- mission 4, Scrap Truce: E phrygian dominant (E F G# A B C D), with D# used strictly as an ascending leading tone to E and never as a free color, so the seventh is never ambiguous; bars 9-14 are a real key change to D harmonic minor (three new pitches: G natural, B-flat, C#, and its own B-flat-to-C# augmented second), pivoted in by a full A7 at bar 8 and returned at bar 14 by a Bb-F-E cadence, so the restatement is an arrival and not a texture change. Form: 2-bar intro stating the bass riff bare with a lead pickup at the end of bar 2; statement and answer (3-7) where the bass anticipates E on step 15 and the whole ensemble then drops the downbeat of the answer bar; 6-bar bridge with a slow sustained counter-voice; 3-bar restatement (call-answer-call) with the counter arpeggio added and a real B7 (D# in the counter over the bass B) as the one functional dominant; a near-silent break at bar 18; a two-bar chromatic riser that actually crosses bars and lands on D#4; and a 4-bar climax. The hook is E-F-G#(held)-rest-A-G#-F-rest-E(held): the augmented second gets the long note and the phrase has two rests, so it has a silhouette you can whistle rather than being an undifferentiated 16th stream; it returns an octave up as E6-C6-G#5 in bars 23-24, where bass, lead and drum lock in unison on a 3+3+3+3+4 grouping (the "bred soldier" moment) after bars 21-22 run that asymmetric bass against a straight machine 16th pulse. Two disagreements with the critiques: I kept phrygian dominant, because the "generic exotic cue" charge is really about the Afro-Cuban tresillo, not the mode, so I cut the tresillo to a single pivot bar and built the rest on 3-against-4 and rotated 2-3-3 cells; and I kept G natural, but only inside the bridge key and inside the A7 that opens it, never adjacent to G# at home. ---

